{ignore}
<p><code>{record "..."}</code> runs its parameter as a query and renders its content once,
with the columns of the first row as fields - the template form of
<code>db ( "RECORD ..." )</code>. The tag name is the command word, so the parameter starts
after the SELECT: <code>{record "* from users where id=5"}</code>.</p>

<p>When the query finds no row the content does not render; an <code>&#64;else&#64;</code> in it gives
the text for that case - "no such customer". Inside, <code>{fields@record}</code> iterates the
columns as <code>$name</code> / <code>$value</code> pairs, for a generic detail view.</p>

<p><code>{record}</code> shares its implementation with <a href="?tag/field">{field}</a> and
<a href="?tag/array">{array}</a>. A value from the request belongs in a placeholder of the
page's PHP - <code>$user = db ( "RECORD * FROM users WHERE id={0}", [ $id ] );</code> - and
then <code>{user}...{/user}</code> renders the same way.</p>
{/ignore}
